[claims]

## (root)
#c1[i5] The anyhow library provides a trait object based error type called anyhow::Error for idiomatic error handling in Rust applications.
  > README.md:9-10 #332995edd222
#c2[i4] The anyhow::Result<T> type is an alias for Result<T, anyhow::Error> and is recommended as the return type for fallible functions.
  > README.md:23-24 #150385c39c7f
#c3[i3] Anyhow supports downcasting by value, shared reference, or mutable reference.
  > README.md:66-67 #e41e0e6ce253
#c4[i3] Setting RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE=1 enables backtraces specifically for errors, while RUST_BACKTRACE=1 enables them for both panics and errors.
  > README.md:85 #e2e2fa1521f8
#c5[i4] The anyhow! macro creates one-off error messages with string interpolation, and the bail! macro provides a shorthand for early returns.
  > README.md:111-112 #f47f319312be
#c6[i4] In no_std mode, the 'std' feature must be disabled in Cargo.toml and a global allocator is required.
  > README.md::"No-std support" #c52f3dc8d20d
#c7[i5] Anyhow is intended for application code where easy error handling is prioritized, whereas thiserror is for designing dedicated library error types.
  > README.md::"Comparison to thiserror" #6fd2d02da2d2

